From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui: HDR only in full screen's rules, tested without AppKit

What: a header with no AppKit in it: whether a frame would show wrong
colours in the surfaces there are (PQ in 8-bit sRGB ones, or sRGB in PQ
ones), when such a held frame is drawn (the hold-off's end, never at or
before now), and the headroom the guest is told while its frames are
8-bit with "HDR only in full screen" on (what the display had while PQ
showed, else its most, capped at 16; after the frames turn PQ not less
until macOS's ramp is done, 3 s). Used by
omacvm-cocoa-present-depth-hold.patch and omacvm-cocoa-hdr-follow.patch
(which explain the why).

Why its own file: so the rules are tested without QEMU or a window
(app/runtime/Tests/display/test-hdr-follow.sh applies this patch to an
empty folder; at build time and in CI).

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's present and display port).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-hdr-follow.c.

diff --git a/ui/omacvm-hdr-follow.h b/ui/omacvm-hdr-follow.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ui/omacvm-hdr-follow.h
@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@
+/*
+ * OmacVM, HDR only in full screen (experimental, ADR 0074): the rules
+ * without AppKit, tested on their own
+ * (app/runtime/Tests/display/test-hdr-follow.c); ui/cocoa.m feeds them.
+ *
+ * The guest's own scanout format is the per-frame signal: an 8-bit scanout
+ * is sRGB, a 10-bit one (GL_RGB10_A2) is PQ while HDR is on. With the app's
+ * switch hdrFullScreenOnly the guest's display agent runs the main window's
+ * output 8-bit sRGB while no window there is in full screen, and today's
+ * 10-bit PQ while one is (omacvm-displays, omacvm-display-sync). So each
+ * frame carries its own colour; two things are left for QEMU:
+ *
+ * 1. A frame is never drawn into surfaces of the other colour. New surfaces
+ *    for a new depth are made at once (omacvm-cocoa-hdr-headroom.patch), but
+ *    at most once a second and not within PRESENT_REALLOC_NS of the last
+ *    ones: in that hold-off a PQ frame went into 8-bit sRGB surfaces (or an
+ *    sRGB one into PQ surfaces) and showed wrong colours until the surfaces
+ *    were made again. Now such a frame is held: the window keeps the last
+ *    frame, and the hold-off's end makes the right surfaces and draws the
+ *    newest frame. Linear surfaces (extra brightness) draw both kinds right
+ *    through their shader, and without HDR both depths are sRGB: no hold.
+ *
+ * 2. The headroom the guest is told while its frames are 8-bit. Those frames
+ *    have no EDR, so the panel's headroom falls to about 1; told that, the
+ *    guest would build its PQ rule for SDR white and the first full screen
+ *    would show HDR flat. So while the frames are SDR the guest is told the
+ *    headroom it had while PQ showed, on this display (else the display's
+ *    most, capped at 16); after the frames turn PQ, not less than that until
+ *    macOS's ramp is done (the current headroom reaches it, or 3 s). The same
+ *    rule as PR #709's omacvm_edr_guest (which can use this one).
+ */
+#ifndef OMACVM_HDR_FOLLOW_H
+#define OMACVM_HDR_FOLLOW_H
+
+#include <math.h>
+#include <stdbool.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include "omacvm-edr.h"
+
+/* A headroom above this (x SDR white) is remembered as the display's HDR headroom. */
+#define OMACVM_HF_SDR_PEAK 1.05
+/* After the frames turn PQ, the guest keeps its value until macOS's ramp is done. */
+#define OMACVM_HF_SETTLE_NS 3000000000LL
+
+/*
+ * Whether a frame (frame_deep: a 10-bit scanout) would show wrong colours in
+ * the surfaces there are (surf_deep: half float, surf_linear: extra
+ * brightness's linear light). pq: 10-bit frames are PQ this run (HDR wanted
+ * and the surfaces tagged).
+ */
+static inline bool omacvm_hf_wrong_colour(bool surf_deep, bool surf_linear, bool frame_deep, bool pq)
+{
+    return pq && !surf_linear && surf_deep != frame_deep;
+}
+
+/*
+ * When a held frame is drawn: at the hold-off's end, never at or before now
+ * (a timer in the past makes QEMU's main loop spin with the BQL held).
+ */
+static inline int64_t omacvm_hf_hold_due(int64_t alloc_ns, int64_t realloc_ns, int64_t now)
+{
+    int64_t due = alloc_ns + realloc_ns;
+
+    return due > now ? due : now + 1000000;
+}
+
+typedef struct OmacvmHfEdr {
+    bool known;         /* a display was seen */
+    uint32_t display;   /* the key: the window's display ... */
+    double potential;   /* ... and its most */
+    double held;        /* the last settled value while PQ showed (> SDR), -1 none */
+    int64_t open_ns;    /* the switch to PQ the settling is for */
+    bool settled;
+} OmacvmHfEdr;
+
+/*
+ * The headroom the guest is told (omacvm-edr.h's rules for the value).
+ * sdr: the frames are 8-bit now. PQ: the current headroom, but not less than
+ * the SDR value until macOS's ramp is done (current reaches it, or 3 s after
+ * open_ns, when the frames turned PQ). A new display (or a new most) forgets
+ * the held value. Never more than the display's most.
+ */
+static inline double omacvm_hf_edr(OmacvmHfEdr *h, uint32_t display, double current, double potential,
+                                   bool use_potential, bool sdr, int64_t open_ns, int64_t now)
+{
+    bool same = h->known && display == h->display &&
+                fabs(potential - h->potential) <= 0.01 * fmax(fabs(h->potential), 1.0);
+    if (!same) {
+        h->known = true;
+        h->display = display;
+        h->potential = potential;
+        h->held = -1;
+        h->settled = false;
+    }
+    double top = omacvm_edr_value(potential, potential, true);
+    double base = h->held > 0 && h->held < top ? h->held : top;
+    if (sdr) {
+        return base;
+    }
+    double v = omacvm_edr_value(current, potential, use_potential);
+    if (open_ns != h->open_ns) {
+        h->open_ns = open_ns;
+        h->settled = false;
+    }
+    if (!h->settled) {
+        if (v < base && now - open_ns < OMACVM_HF_SETTLE_NS) {
+            return base;
+        }
+        h->settled = true;
+    }
+    if (v > OMACVM_HF_SDR_PEAK) {
+        h->held = v;
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
+#endif
